    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Baekje grew from a Mahan statelet on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> into a kingdom of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Ungjin</a> and Sabi.</li><li>Its court was celebrated for refined Buddhist art, metalwork and stone pagodas, seen in King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muryeong-of-baekje">Muryeong</a>'s tomb and the Baekje <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burner.</li><li>Baekje passed Chinese writing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and skilled craftsmen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato Japan</a>, its closest ally, before falling to Silla and the Tang in 660.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muryeong-of-baekje">Muryeong</a>'s tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Gongju</a>, found unlooted in 1971, held an epitaph stone naming the king, a rare case in which a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> royal tomb can be identified with certainty.</p></aside>
    </section>

