    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/onjo-of-baekje"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/onjo-of-baekje.jpg" alt="Onjo of Baekje" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/onjo-of-baekje">Onjo of Baekje</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%B4%88%EB%93%B1%EB%8C%80%ED%95%9C%EC%97%AD%EC%82%AC_%EC%A0%9C2%ED%8E%B8_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original: Jeong Inho Transcription: History Net, National Institute of Korean History, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baekje arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> basin, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, among the walled-town communities of the Mahan confederacy, which Chinese sources of the 3rd century list as some fifty small polities in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Korean tradition, preserved in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a></em>, holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onjo-of-baekje">Onjo</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-goguryeo">Jumong</a>, the founder of Goguryeo, came south with followers and founded the kingdom in 18 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiryeseong">Wiryeseong</a>; another version credits his elder brother Biryu. The royal house claimed descent from Buyeo, a claim later expressed in the royal surname Buyeo and in the alternative state name Nambuyeo, 'Southern Buyeo'. Archaeologically, the earthen walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pungnap-toseong">Pungnap Toseong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongchon-toseong">Mongchon Toseong</a> in south-eastern Seoul, raised around the 3rd century CE, mark the emergence of a sizeable royal centre, and most historians date Baekje's development into a true kingdom to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goi-of-baekje">King Goi</a> (traditionally 234–286) or later. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geunchogo-of-baekje">Geunchogo</a> (r. 346–375) is generally credited with absorbing the remaining Mahan polities towards the south-west coast. He opened relations with the Eastern Jin court in 372, and in 371 his army killed the Goguryeo king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gogugwon-of-goguryeo">Gogugwon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, making Baekje for a time the strongest of the Korean kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iksan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iksan.jpg" alt="Iksan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iksan">Iksan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iksan_-_Young_Deung_Dong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Iksanirish, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baekje's core lay in the fertile plains of the Han, Geum and Yeongsan river basins, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> grown in irrigated paddies, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and barley, formed the basis of the economy. Society was led by a small group of great families; the Chinese <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> shu</em> names eight leading clans, including the Sa, Yeon, Hae, Jin and Mok, who supplied the highest officials and royal consorts. Below them stood free farmers liable to taxes, military service and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour, and unfree people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, brought from the Eastern Jin by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marananta">Marananta</a> in 384, became the faith of the court, and the Sabi period saw great temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongnimsa">Jeongnimsa</a>, whose five-storey stone pagoda still stands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mireuksa">Mireuksa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iksan">Iksan</a>, one of the largest temple complexes of its age, whose reliquary inscription dates its western pagoda to 639. Baekje craftsmen were renowned: the tomb of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muryeong-of-baekje">Muryeong</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Gongju</a>, found intact in 1971, yielded gold crown ornaments, jewellery and Chinese ceramics, and the gilt-bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burner discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neungsan-ri">Neungsan-ri</a> in 1993 is among the masterpieces of East Asian metalwork. Sea routes linked Baekje with the southern Chinese courts, the Gaya states and the Japanese archipelago, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>, ceramics and books from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and carrying Baekje goods, scholars and artisans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hwangsanbeol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hwangsanbeol.jpg" alt="Battle of Hwangsanbeol" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hwangsanbeol">Battle of Hwangsanbeol</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Kingdoms_of_Korea_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baekje's armies were led by aristocratic commanders and raised from provincial levies, supported by earthen and stone fortresses along its frontiers and by ships that controlled the coastal crossings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a>. For most of its history Baekje fought on two fronts. Its early wars against Goguryeo culminated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geunchogo-of-baekje">Geunchogo</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 371, but under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great">Gwanggaeto the Great</a> Goguryeo struck back, taking dozens of Baekje fortresses in 396 and forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asin-of-baekje">King Asin</a> to submit. The decisive blow came in 475, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangsu-of-goguryeo">King Jangsu</a> of Goguryeo captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a> and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaero-of-baekje">King Gaero</a>, compelling the court to flee south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Ungjin</a> (Gongju). From 433 Baekje was allied with Silla against Goguryeo, and in 551 their combined forces recovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> basin, only for Silla to seize it for itself in 553. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje">Seong</a> (r. 523–554) attacked Silla in revenge and was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gwansanseong">Battle of Gwansanseong</a> in 554, a defeat in which, according to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a></em>, nearly 30,000 Baekje soldiers died. Baekje regained the initiative under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uija-of-baekje">Uija</a>, who took some forty Silla fortresses and the stronghold of Daeya in 642, but in 660 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyebaek">Gyebaek</a> fell with his last army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hwangsanbeol">Hwangsanbeol</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a>, and the capital was lost.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi.jpg" alt="Samguk sagi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samguk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gomanari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baekje was a hereditary monarchy in which the king, of the Buyeo lineage, shared power with the great aristocratic clans. Tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goi-of-baekje">King Goi</a> with a system of sixteen official ranks headed by the <em>jwapyeong</em>, distinguished by the colours of official robes; whatever its exact date, a graded bureaucracy of this kind was in place by the 6th century. Early local control rested on the <em>damno</em>, districts often entrusted to royal kinsmen. After the move to Sabi in 538 the government was organised into twenty-two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, divided between those serving the royal household and those handling state affairs; the capital was divided into five <em>bu</em> and the territory into five <em>bang</em>, or regions, each governed from a fortress by an appointed commander. Kings sought legitimacy through investiture by Chinese emperors, from whom they received titles of general and king, and Baekje envoys appear at the courts of the Eastern Jin, Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Liang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang. Records were kept in Chinese: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a></em> reports that the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/go-heung">Go Heung</a> compiled a history called <em>Seogi</em> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geunchogo-of-baekje">Geunchogo</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muryeong-of-baekje">Muryeong</a>'s epitaph stone, which records his death in 523 in Chinese-style dating, and the many inscribed wooden tablets excavated at Sabi show a literate court and administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/asuka-dera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asuka-dera.jpg" alt="Asuka-dera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/asuka-dera">Asuka-dera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asuka-dera,_sanmon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saigen Jiro, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baekje's most enduring influence lay across the sea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Its close alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato court</a>, sealed by exchanges of princes and envoys, made it a main route by which Chinese-derived learning, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and technology reached the Japanese archipelago. Japanese chronicles relate that the scholar Wani came from Baekje bringing the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a></em> and, anachronistically, the <em>Thousand Character Classic</em>, and that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje">Seong</a> sent a Buddhist image and sutras to the Yamato court, in 552 according to the <em>Nihon shoki</em>, though another Japanese source gives 538, the date most often preferred today. Baekje monks, carpenters, tile-makers, painters and physicians helped build Japan's first great temples, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuka-dera">Asuka-dera</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-branched-sword">Seven-Branched Sword</a> kept at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isonokami-shrine">Isonokami Shrine</a>, inscribed with a date usually read as 369, is generally interpreted as a gift from a Baekje king to a Wa ruler. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, Baekje's art, marked by gentle Buddhist sculpture such as the rock-carved triad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seosan">Seosan</a>, and its stone pagodas influenced Silla and later Korean styles. After 660 thousands of Baekje refugees, including members of the royal family, settled in Japan, where the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenko">Zenkō</a> received the surname Kudara no Konikishi and refugee experts helped build Korean-style mountain fortresses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baekje-historic-areas">Baekje Historic Areas</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Gongju</a>, Buyeo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iksan">Iksan</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a> in 2015.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baekgang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-baekgang.jpg" alt="Battle of Baekgang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baekgang">Battle of Baekgang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Battle_of_Baekgang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stillskywkpd, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Baekje's fall resulted from its long struggle with Silla, which after taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> basin in 553 gained direct sea access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and courted the Tang. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uija-of-baekje">Uija</a>'s successful offensives of the 640s, including the capture of Daeya fortress in 642, in which the son-in-law of the Silla noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muyeol-of-silla">Kim Chunchu</a> was killed, pushed Silla to seek Tang help, and Kim Chunchu travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 648 to negotiate an alliance. In 660 a Tang fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-dingfang">Su Dingfang</a> landed at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geum-river">Geum River</a> while Silla forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a> advanced overland and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyebaek">Gyebaek</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hwangsanbeol">Hwangsanbeol</a>. Sabi fell in the seventh month of 660; King Uija surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Ungjin</a> and was taken with his family and many officials to the Tang court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, where he died. Later Korean histories blamed his luxury and neglect of loyal advisers, a view probably coloured by the victors. A restoration movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boksin">Boksin</a> and the monk Dochim, with Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyeo-pung">Buyeo Pung</a> brought back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, held out for three years with Yamato support, until a Tang and Silla fleet destroyed the Japanese navy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baekgang">Battle of Baekgang</a> in 663 and the last fortress, Imjonseong, fell. The Tang briefly administered the former kingdom through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ungjin-commandery">Ungjin Area Command</a> before Silla absorbed its lands by 676.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Baekje, the south-western kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-of-korea">Korea's Three Kingdoms</a> period, created a refined Buddhist and literate court culture and served as the main bridge between the Korean peninsula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, transmitting writing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and craftsmanship to Yamato before Silla and the Tang destroyed it in 660.</em></p>

